

## Light Mode Thrust (Radiation Pressure)
Standard solar sail force for perfect reflector:  
$$ F = 2 \frac{P}{c} A \cos^2 \alpha $$ [4][1]
- Solar constant $$ P = 1361 $$ W/m² at 1 AU  
- Speed of light $$ c = 3 \times 10^8 $$ m/s  
- Sail area $$ A = 100 $$ m² (SLaSS design)  
- Pitch angle $$ \alpha = 0^\circ $$ (max thrust, $$ \cos^2 \alpha = 1 $$)  

Pressure: $$ p = 2 \frac{1361}{3 \times 10^8} = 9.07 \times 10^{-6} $$ N/m² = 9.07 μN/m²  
Total force: $$ F = 9.07 \times 10^{-6} \times 100 = 9.07 \times 10^{-4} $$ N [4][2]

## Acceleration
CubeSat mass $$ m = 10 $$ kg (typical):  
$$ a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{9.07 \times 10^{-4}}{10} = 9.07 \times 10^{-5} \, \mathrm{m/s^2} \approx 0.01 \, \mathrm{mm/s^2} $$ [11]
Δv over 1 year ($$ t = 3.156 \times 10^7 $$ s): $$ \Delta v = a t = 2.86 $$ km/s

## Shadow Mode Thrust (PCM Ablation Estimate)
Thermal ablation (conservative 10% light thrust): $$ F_\mathrm{shadow} = 1 \, \mu\mathrm{N/m^2} $$  
Total: $$ F = 1 \times 10^{-6} \times 100 = 10^{-4} $$ N  
Exhaust velocity $$ v_e = 2000 $$ m/s (solid thermal):  
Mass flow: $$ \dot{m} = \frac{F}{v_e} = 5 \times 10^{-8} $$ kg/s  
1-hour shadow (3600 s): $$ \Delta m = 1.8 \times 10^{-4} $$ kg = 0.18 g [12]

## Lattice Reflectivity Gain
Perforated design: $$ \eta = 0.99 $$ (vs. 0.90 standard) [13]
Thrust boost: $$ F_\mathrm{lattice} = 1.1 \times F_\mathrm{std} $$  
Mass savings: 30% via voids ($$ m_\mathrm{sail} = 0.7 \times m_\mathrm{std} $$)

## Fuel Savings
Traditional shadow Δv = 1 km/s, $$ m_p = m_0 (1 - e^{-\Delta v / v_e}) $$, $$ v_e = 3000 $$ m/s: ~33% propellant fraction  
SLaSS hybrid: <1% PCM over mission → 90% savings [14]

## Trajectory Example (Earth-Mars)
Total Δv ≈ 5-6 km/s; SLaSS continuous low-thrust halves time vs. pure sail (avoids umbra stalls) [15]

